# NO merge driver for src/lib/changelog.ts. It must CONFLICT.
#
# 2026-09-15 this file carried `src/lib/changelog.ts merge=union`, on the
# reasoning that the changelog is append-only and sorted at runtime, so two
# branches adding entries "have no reason to conflict". The comment claimed it
# was "safe for ADDING entries". **That is false, and it broke a release.**
#
# `merge=union` works on LINES and knows nothing about nesting. Both sides
# insert right after `const RELEASES: Release[] = [`, and the inserted blocks
# share plenty of common lines (`    version: "…",`, `    date: "…",`, `      {`,
# `        kind: "fix",`). Git aligns on those, so the union keeps one side's
# opening brace and the other side's closing one and splices the two entries
# together:
#
#       {
#         kind: "improvement",
#         text: "…",
#     version: "2.372.6",        <- the other entry starts MID-OBJECT
#
# Everything after that point is unbalanced. Measured on auto/COSMOS-176 vs
# main, 2026-09-16:
#
#   git merge-file          → exit 1, 1 conflict marker   (what we WANT)
#   git merge-file --union  → exit 0, silently corrupt    (what we HAD)
#
# The corrupt file then fails the post-rebase typecheck with a cluster of
# TS1005s near the END of the array — far from the damage — and the approve
# parks instead of shipping. That is exactly what happened to COSMOS-176:
#   COSMOS-176 approve → merge FAILED (post-rebase typecheck failed …
#   src/lib/changelog.ts(6076,42): error TS1005: ',' expected.)
#
# The union driver is not merely unnecessary here, it is HARMFUL: by exiting 0
# it hides the file from `git diff --diff-filter=U`, so the ship path's
# mechanical resolver never sees it. That resolver already handles this file
# correctly — `src/lib/changelog.ts` is in VERSION_RACE_TRIO, so a conflict is
# resolved by taking main's copy and re-applying the branch's entry through
# `prependChangelogEntry`, which produces valid TypeScript by construction.
#
# So: let it conflict. The conflict is the mechanism, not the problem.
